@anon.984efe779a4077b66be6415f964eeb42.2060 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4636 x i8] }> <{ [4636 x i8] c"Arranges content in a grid.\0A\0AThe grid element allows you to arrange content in a grid. You can define the\0Anumber of rows and columns, as well as the size of the gutters between them.\0AThere are multiple sizing modes for columns and rows that can be used to\0Acreate complex layouts.\0A\0AWhile the grid and table elements work very similarly, they are intended for\0Adifferent use cases and carry different semantics. The grid element is\0Aintended for presentational and layout purposes, while the\0A[`{table}`]($table) element is intended for, in broad terms, presenting\0Amultiple related data points. In the future, Typst will annotate its output\0Asuch that screenreaders will annouce content in `table` as tabular while a\0Agrid's content will be announced no different than multiple content blocks\0Ain the document flow. Set and show rules on one of these elements do not\0Aaffect the other.\0A\0AA grid's sizing is determined by the track sizes specified in the arguments.\0ABecause each of the sizing parameters accepts the same values, we will\0Aexplain them just once, here. Each sizing argument accepts an array of\0Aindividual track sizes. A track size is either:\0A\0A- `{auto}`: The track will be sized to fit its contents. It will be at most\0A  as large as the remaining space. If there is more than one `{auto}` track\0A  width, and together they claim more than the available space, the `{auto}`\0A  tracks will fairly distribute the available space among themselves.\0A\0A- A fixed or relative length (e.g. `{10pt}` or `{20% - 1cm}`): The track\0A  will be exactly of this size.\0A\0A- A fractional length (e.g. `{1fr}`): Once all other tracks have been sized,\0A  the remaining space will be divided among the fractional tracks according\0A  to their fractions. For example, if there are two fractional tracks, each\0A  with a fraction of `{1fr}`, they will each take up half of the remaining\0A  space.\0A\0ATo specify a single track, the array can be omitted in favor of a single\0Avalue. To specify multiple `{auto}` tracks, enter the number of tracks\0Ainstead of an array. For example, `columns:` `{3}` is equivalent to\0A`columns:` `{(auto, auto, auto)}`.\0A\0A# Examples\0AThe example below demonstrates the different track sizing options. It also\0Ashows how you can use [`grid.cell`]($grid.cell) to make an individual cell\0Aspan two grid tracks.\0A\0A```example\0A// We use `rect` to emphasize the\0A// area of cells.\0A#set rect(\0A  inset: 8pt,\0A  fill: rgb(\22e4e5ea\22),\0A  width: 100%,\0A)\0A\0A#grid(\0A  columns: (60pt, 1fr, 2fr),\0A  rows: (auto, 60pt),\0A  gutter: 3pt,\0A  rect[Fixed width, auto height],\0A  rect[1/3 of the remains],\0A  rect[2/3 of the remains],\0A  rect(height: 100%)[Fixed height],\0A  grid.cell(\0A    colspan: 2,\0A    image(\22tiger.jpg\22, width: 100%),\0A  ),\0A)\0A```\0A\0AYou can also [spread]($arguments/#spreading) an array of strings or content\0Ainto a grid to populate its cells.\0A\0A```example\0A#grid(\0A  columns: 5,\0A  gutter: 5pt,\0A  ..range(25).map(str)\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Styling the grid\0AThe grid's appearance can be customized through different parameters. These\0Aare the most important ones:\0A\0A- [`fill`]($grid.fill) to give all cells a background\0A- [`align`]($grid.align) to change how cells are aligned\0A- [`inset`]($grid.inset) to optionally add internal padding to each cell\0A- [`stroke`]($grid.stroke) to optionally enable grid lines with a certain\0A  stroke\0A\0AIf you need to override one of the above options for a single cell, you can\0Ause the [`grid.cell`]($grid.cell) element. Likewise, you can override\0Aindividual grid lines with the [`grid.hline`]($grid.hline) and\0A[`grid.vline`]($grid.vline) elements.\0A\0AAlternatively, if you need the appearance options to depend on a cell's\0Aposition (column and row), you may specify a function to `fill` or `align`\0Aof the form `(column, row) => value`. You may also use a show rule on\0A[`grid.cell`]($grid.cell) - see that element's examples or the examples\0Abelow for more information.\0A\0ALocating most of your styling in set and show rules is recommended, as it\0Akeeps the grid's or table's actual usages clean and easy to read. It also\0Aallows you to easily change the grid's appearance in one place.\0A\0A## Stroke styling precedence\0AThere are three ways to set the stroke of a grid cell: through\0A[`{grid.cell}`'s `stroke` field]($grid.cell.stroke), by using\0A[`{grid.hline}`]($grid.hline) and [`{grid.vline}`]($grid.vline), or by\0Asetting the [`{grid}`'s `stroke` field]($grid.stroke). When multiple of\0Athese settings are present and conflict, the `hline` and `vline` settings\0Atake the highest precedence, followed by the `cell` settings, and finally\0Athe `grid` settings.\0A\0AFurthermore, strokes of a repeated grid header or footer will take\0Aprecedence over regular cell strokes." }>, align 1
